t we need to swap the second and third rows.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the first row to the second row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1+(-1)(1) & -1+(-1)(0) & 0+(-1)(0) & 3+(-1)(2) \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186409502 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186409503 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186409504 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186409509 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186409510 :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186059582 :question/problem "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where \n![](images/56/56fd7e343edf6c26f36718a30f899af6c8eaf0cad5f6b07c90ffa93c19df6346.png)"] [17592186059582 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nNow, applying the operation $R_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4+1\\cdot (-4) & 1+1\\cdot (-2) & 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation \n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1.\n$$"] [17592186060199 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -14\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -14\\end{bmatrix}$?"] [17592186057655 :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{3}"] [17592186057656 :answer/value "R_{1}:=R_{1} + \\frac{1}{4}R_{2}"] [17592186057657 :answer/value "R_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}R_{2}"] [17592186057658 :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1}"] [17592186057659 :answer/value "R_{1}↔R_{2}"] [17592186060203 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $X$ into the matrix $Y$, where\n$X = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, Y = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$?"] [17592186057679 :answer/value "R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-3)R_{1}"] [17592186057680 :answer/value "R_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}R_{3}"] [17592186057681 :answer/value "R_{1}↔R_{3}"] [17592186057682 :answer/value "R_{1}↔R_{2}"] [17592186057683 :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + 3R_{1}"] [17592186265820 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186265820 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $2$nd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $3$rd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $3$rd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {R}_{3}.\n$$"] [17592186263254 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}"] [17592186263822 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {R}_{3}"] [17592186264042 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}"] [17592186264244 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}"] [17592186264958 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{10})\\cdot {R}_{3}"] [17592186265822 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix  $C$ into the matrix $D$, where \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186265822 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $1$st row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $1$st row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=3\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=3\\cdot {R}_{1}.\n$$"] [17592186263237 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}"] [17592186264009 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {R}_{1}"] [17592186264013 :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}"] [17592186264273 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=3\\cdot {R}_{1}"] [17592186264700 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{4}{7})\\cdot {R}_{2}"] [17592186265824 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $E$ into the matrix $F$, where\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,F=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186265824 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row is the same in both matrices. The only rows that have changed are the $2$nd row and the $3$rd row.   \n\nBy considering the given row operations that change both the $2$nd and the $3$rd row, we notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}.\n$$"] [17592186263975 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}"] [17592186264103 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=(-\\frac{8}{3})\\cdot {R}_{3}"] [17592186264204 :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}"] [17592186264343 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1}"] [17592186264901 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=(-\\frac{3}{8})\\cdot {R}_{2}"] [17592186265825 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $X$ into the matrix $W$, where\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186265825 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $1$st row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $1$st row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =W.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{3}.\n$$"] [17592186263043 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{3}"] [17592186263070 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}"] [17592186264077 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{3}"] [17592186264229 :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}"] [17592186264816 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1}"] [17592186287989 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $S$ into the matrix $T$, where\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}60 & 0 & 120 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 40 \\\\ 15 & 0 & 30\\end{bmatrix},\\,T=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 & 30 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 40 \\\\ 15 & 0 & 30\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186287989 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd and $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $1$st row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that changes the $1$st  row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\begin{bmatrix}60 & 0 & 120 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 40 \\\\ 15 & 0 & 30\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 & 30 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 40 \\\\ 15 & 0 & 30\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =T.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}.\n$$"] [17592186284920 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}"] [17592186285336 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}"] [17592186285564 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}"] [17592186287030 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=(-2){R}_{1}"] [17592186287104 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}"] [17592186341877 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $U$ into the matrix $V$, where\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186341877 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row is the same in both matrices. The only rows that have changed are the $1$st row and the $3$rd row.   \n\nBy considering the given row operations that change both the $1$st and the $3$rd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =V.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}.\n$$"] [17592186339517 :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}"] [17592186339527 :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{1}"] [17592186340183 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}"] [17592186340491 :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}"] [17592186341337 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}"] [17592186341878 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $X$ into the matrix $W$, where\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186341878 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $2$nd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =W.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}.\n$$"] [17592186339312 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}"] [17592186339352 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3}"] [17592186339916 :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}"] [17592186340659 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}"] [17592186341094 :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}"] [17592186341879 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $K$ into the matrix $L$, where\n\n$$\nK=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,L=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186341879 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $2$nd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}.\n$$"] [17592186339278 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}"] [17592186339781 :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}"] [17592186340430 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}"] [17592186341181 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}"] [17592186341228 :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{1}"] [17592186059583 :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/59/59ee8e91d058e347238320a2bd5496630f33534564f67f901d2ddf89000f0108.png) which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0?$"] [17592186059583 :question/worked-solution "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0.$\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0,$ because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0.$\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1.$ If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22},$ we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1.\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1+(-5)\\cdot 3 & 5+(-5)\\cdot 1 & 6+(-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1,\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 -5 R_1.\n$$"] [17592186060202 :question/problem "Given that\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{22}=0?$"] [17592186060202 :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}.\n$$"] [17592186285397 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2}"] [17592186286481 :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}"] [17592186286692 :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}"] [17592186286938 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}"] [17592186286983 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}"] [17592186060204 :question/problem "Given that\n$X = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes $a_{21} = 0$?"] [17592186057685 :answer/value "R_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}R_{2}"] [17592186057686 :answer/value "R_{2}:=(-1)R_{2}"] [17592186057687 :answer/value "R_{3}↔R_{1}"] [17592186057688 :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1}"] [17592186057689 :answer/value "R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1}"] [17592186265873 :question/problem "Given that\n$X=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 10 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{13}=0?$"] [17592186265873 :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 10 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}.\n$$"] [17592186263251 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}"] [17592186263619 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=(-2){R}_{1}"] [17592186263683 :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}"] [17592186263910 :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}"] [17592186264085 :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}"] [17592186265874 :question/problem "Given \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{32}=0?$"] [17592186265874 :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$"] [17592186263522 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}"] [17592186263630 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=(-2){R}_{3}"] [17592186264210 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}"] [17592186264720 :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}"] [17592186265167 :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}"] [17592186265876 :question/problem "Given \n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$,\n which of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{23}=0?$"] [17592186265876 :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}.\n$$"] [17592186263141 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}"] [17592186263296 :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}"] [17592186263929 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}"] [17592186264588 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}"] [17592186264896 :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{1}"] [17592186265877 :question/problem "Given \n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{32}=0?$"] [17592186265877 :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -20 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2}.\n$$"] [17592186264368 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=(-3){R}_{3}"] [17592186264551 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}"] [17592186264856 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2}"] [17592186265090 :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}"] [17592186265116 :answer/value "{R}_{3}↔{R}_{2}"] [17592186287982 :question/problem "Given \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 12 & 24\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{22}=0?$"] [17592186287982 :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 12 & 24\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12 & 24\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$"] [17592186284835 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}"] [17592186284864 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}"] [17592186285279 :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{1}"] [17592186285632 :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}"] [17592186286086 :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}"] [17592186287988 :question/problem "Given \n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$,\n which of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{31}=0?$"] [17592186287988 :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}.\n$$"] [17592186285473 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}"] [17592186285644 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}"] [17592186285983 :answer/value "{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}"] [17592186286112 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}"] [17592186286535 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}"] [17592186341880 :question/problem "Given that\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\,-4 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,4 \\\\ 2 & \\,2 & \\,1\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{33}=0?$"] [17592186341880 :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation    \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\,-4 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,4 \\\\ 2 & \\,2 & \\,1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\,-4 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,4 \\\\ 3 & \\,0 & \\,0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}.\n$$"] [17592186339145 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}"] [17592186339744 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=(-1){R}_{3}"] [17592186339953 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}"] [17592186340431 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}"] [17592186341327 :answer/value "{R}_{3}↔{R}_{2}"] [17592186341881 :question/problem "Given that\n$S=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{33}=0?$"] [17592186341881 :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}.\n$$"] [17592186339741 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}"] [17592186340019 :answer/value "{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}"] [17592186340032 :answer/value "{R}_{3}↔{R}_{2}"] [17592186340112 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2}"] [17592186340508 :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}"] [17592186130339 :tutorial/content "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/10/1074870d255109821b5f36eb517f22531efbd8f5069f05c3de3f111b38befd02.svg)\n\nThe first non-zero entry on each row (from left to right) is called the **leading entry** of that row.\n\n- In the matrix $A,$ the leading entries are $a_{11}=2,$ $a_{22}=3,$ $a_{32}=1,$ and $a_{43}=5.$\n\n- In the matrix $B,$ the leading entries are $b_{11}=1,$ $b_{22}=3,$ $b_{33}=-2,$ and $b_{44}=2.$ The $5$th row has no leading entry.\n\nThe leading entries are highlighted below.\n\n![](images/6b/6b1d99269c847f7c6bc0558da347fb300dba5fa6caf373cd1ed5cc5ebacb1649.svg)"] [17592186128925 :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n![](images/27/2701e789f5d3f6fc48fda87706add41ccb59c92d5bae4a76e84ac1abfdce6f07.svg)"] [17592186128925 :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/d8/d8725937a4f5f1cfcd36cbe4871a872187df071b54c15d2e29d9b1e3c5f9b731.svg)\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/5e/5e3142b1ad4bb219db5d5b144e47fe8d1fa8d1f92c300c6f60fc145cf9424900.svg)\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/93/9331fdbede7da49e2dbbc66aac0b3084b1c7acca1ac7e789eded304956a18500.svg)\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/76/76e7add6349e0b2fceddf094ac6e3e8cfd0bf1bb43a3fbee0c143b256b81f1c5.svg)"] [17592186129771 :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 3 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 5\\end{array}\\right],\\,B=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}3 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right],\\,C=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186129771 :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 3 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 5\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}3 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since ${c}_{22}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are in row echelon form."] [17592186459951 :answer/value "false"] [17592186459952 :answer/value "true"] [17592186459953 :answer/value "false"] [17592186459954 :answer/value "true"] [17592186459955 :answer/value "false"] [17592186459956 :answer/value "true"] [17592186129774 :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n$A = \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 3\\end{array}\\right], B = \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6\\end{array}\\right], C = \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}4 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]$."] [17592186128126 :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only"] [17592186128127 :answer/value "$C$ only"] [17592186128128 :answer/value "$B$ only"] [17592186128129 :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only"] [17592186128130 :answer/value "$A$ only"] [17592186262994 :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}4 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right],\\,B=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 7 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right],\\,C=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 9 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186262994 :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}4 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 7 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 