$3$rd, and $4$th only"] [17592186323715 :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186323715 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $2$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns."] [17592186321148 :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd"] [17592186321356 :answer/value "$2$nd only"] [17592186321948 :answer/value "$3$rd only"] [17592186322959 :answer/value "$1$st only"] [17592186323048 :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only"] [17592186342045 :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186342045 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $B$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $B$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns."] [17592186339233 :answer/value "$1$st, $3$rd only"] [17592186339565 :answer/value "$3$rd only"] [17592186340371 :answer/value "$2$nd, and $4$th only"] [17592186340632 :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd only"] [17592186340642 :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd and $4$th"] [17592186342046 :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186342046 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $4$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $2$nd, $3$rd, $5$th and $6$th columns."] [17592186340666 :answer/value "$2$nd only"] [17592186340750 :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, and $6$th only"] [17592186340820 :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, $5$th, and $6$th only"] [17592186341057 :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, $5$th, and $6$th only"] [17592186341068 :answer/value "$1$st and $6$th only"] [17592186367373 :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -6 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186367373 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -6 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $4$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $2$nd, $3$rd, $5$th, and $6$th columns."] [17592186367375 :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, $5$th, and $6$th only"] [17592186367376 :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th only"] [17592186367377 :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, $4$th, and $5$th only"] [17592186367378 :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th only"] [17592186367379 :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, $4$th, and $6$th only"] [17592186400590 :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186400590 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{4} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns."] [17592186400592 :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only"] [17592186400593 :answer/value "$1$st and $4$th only"] [17592186400594 :answer/value "$1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th only"] [17592186400595 :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd only"] [17592186400596 :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th only"] [17592186402448 :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186402448 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $2$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns."] [17592186402450 :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only"] [17592186402451 :answer/value "$3$rd only"] [17592186402452 :answer/value "$1$st only"] [17592186402453 :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd"] [17592186402454 :answer/value "$2$nd only"] [17592186402461 :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186402461 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns."] [17592186402463 :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only"] [17592186402464 :answer/value "$3$rd only"] [17592186402465 :answer/value "$2$nd and $3$rd only"] [17592186402466 :answer/value "$3$rd and $4$th only"] [17592186402467 :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th only"] [17592186403682 :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186403682 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{4} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $4$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th columns."] [17592186403684 :answer/value "$1$st and $4$th only"] [17592186403685 :answer/value "$2$nd and $3$rd only"] [17592186403686 :answer/value "$1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th only"] [17592186403687 :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th only"] [17592186403688 :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th only"] [17592186116493 :tutorial/content "We can use Gaussian elimination to find the general solutions of systems with any number of equations and variables. For instance, consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=1, \\\\ y+3z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has the augmented matrix\n![](images/8a/8a8452587098eed9b697c3999c356359b949429f29ce16af2c81cf728be9480e.svg)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\, & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4z &=1 \\\\ y+3z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe see that the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable $z.$ From the second equation, we get $y=-3z,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x=1-4z.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1-4z, \\\\ y &=-3z, \\\\ z &=z.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-4z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115070 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/40/406033f059dc4438ea55d43d2bd00b3b79bb99365a2e4846c995f4a447acfd82.svg)\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186115070 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](images/be/bed2784d17ee63b805917b492052daa7ef079c1682bbc78468eb10f314a92b1f.svg)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/91/91175ab97472067cd14ffff740bb3a98718436f1e94de7a4a6d7fdfb1b35b81f.svg)\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1-5y \\\\ y &=y \\\\ z &=3,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115751 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x + y + 2z &=19 \\\\ 3x + y + 2z &=31\\end{aligned}\\right.$.\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186113473 :answer/value "No solution"] [17592186113474 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ 13 - 2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}, z ∈ (- ∞, ∞)"] [17592186113475 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ 13 - 2z \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}, z ∈ (- ∞, ∞)"] [17592186113476 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 13 - 2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}, z ∈ (- ∞, ∞)"] [17592186113477 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 - z \\\\ 13 - 2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}, z ∈ (- ∞, ∞)"] [17592186115830 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3z &=-2 \\\\ x+y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186115830 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3z &=-2 \\\\ y-3z &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2-3z \\\\ y &=2+3z \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the latter can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-3z \\\\ 2+3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186113918 :answer/value "No solution"] [17592186492423 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2+3z \\\\ 2+3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186492497 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2-3z \\\\ 2+3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186492510 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2-3z \\\\ 3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186492582 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-3z \\\\ 2-3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186266111 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186266111 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}-5{x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=3{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=5{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{3} &={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186263546 :answer/value "No solution"] [17592186264378 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186264677 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1-3{x}_{3} \\\\ 1-5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186265070 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ 1-5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186265244 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186266112 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186266112 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-5{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} &={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186263196 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186263881 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{3}+4 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186264574 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186264787 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186265011 :answer/value "No solution"] [17592186302108 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+2y &=5 \\\\ -10x-10y &=-25 \\\\ 8x+8y &=20.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186302108 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y &=y,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186300051 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{5}-2 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186300189 :answer/value "No solution"] [17592186300539 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186300774 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5-y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186300815 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}+x \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186302193 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186302193 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now   \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ Since we have that ${x}_{1}=4$,  we only need to find ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-6-2{x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299936 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186300041 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 6+{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186300054 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186300311 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186300994 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 6-2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186326924 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4z &=2 \\\\ 4x+y-5z &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186326924 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2z &=1 \\\\ y+3z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+2z \\\\ y &=-3z \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186324637 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2z \\\\ 4z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186324720 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186324977 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2+4z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186325282 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2+4z \\\\ 1-3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186325961 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1+4z \\\\ 3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186400625 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+8y &=-6 \\\\ -4x-16y &=12 \\\\ 6x+24y &=-18.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186400625 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-4y-3 \\\\ y &=y,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186400627 :answer/value "No solution"] [17592186400628 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186400629 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4y+3 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186400630 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186400631 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3-4y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186402009 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186402009 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+10{x}_{3} &=-8 \\\\ {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} &={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186402011 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8-10{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186402012 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8-{x}_{3} \\\\ 3+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186402013 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8-8{x}_{3} \\\\ 6-6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186402014 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8-6{x}_{3} \\\\ 10+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186402015 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8-{x}_{3} \\\\ 6+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186402756 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z &=2 \\\\ y-z &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186402756 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y-z &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=z-1 \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186402758 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186402759 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186402760 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-z \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186402761 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186402762 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186417081 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=2 \\\\ y+2z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186417081 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-z &=2 \\\\ y+2z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2+z \\\\ y &=-2z \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the latter can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186417072 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ -z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186417073 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186417075 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186417076 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186417077 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186116494 :tutorial/content "Recall that a system of linear equations is called **consistent** if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, if there are no solutions, the system is called **inconsistent**. \n\nFor a consistent system, \n\n- the system is called **consistent independent** if the solution is unique, while\n\n- the system is called **consistent dependent** if there are infinitely many solutions.\n\n![](images/f5/f5b808eac6e1d865b13b68ce2a4f7d317560ae3965928e478531fcd2148c2c6b.png)"] [17592186115071 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y &=1, \\\\ 4x+ky &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186115071 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/6f/6fa4508463b059ce8ef9a5cfa720de9463e41ecdbfb16f4460870bb6fce444bc.svg)\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n![](images/21/215ff061dc7584055bf607f48d0048cb6bde4dcb1ed028fbf4c3a4bd47d89c25.svg)\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=-5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$"] [17592186115753 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y &=12 \\\\ x+ky &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186115753 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4-3k & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+ky &=4 \\\\ (4-3k)y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{4}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{4}{3}.$"] [17592186300213 :answer/value "k=0"] [17592186300447 :answer/value "k=\\frac{4}{3}"] [17592186300503 :answer/value "k=\\frac{3}{4}"] [17592186300522 :answer/value "k=-4"] [17592186301203 :answer/value "k=-\\frac{4}{3}"] [17592186115831 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y &=1 \\\\ 6x+(k+3)y &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186115831 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & k+3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & k+3 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k-6 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y &=1 \\\\ (k-6)y &=-8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=6,$ then the second equation is $0=-8,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=6.$"] [17592186425939 :answer/value "k=9"] [17592186425941 :answer/value "k=6"] [17592186425942 :answer/value "k=4"] [17592186425943 :answer/value "k=-2"] [17592186425945 :answer/value "k=7"] [17592186266116 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+2y &=1 \\\\ 6x+(k+9)y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186266116 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & k+9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & k+9 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+13 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+2y &=1 \\\\ (k+13)y &=7.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-13,$ then the second equation is $0=7,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-13.$"] [17592186263232 :answer/value "k=-13"] [17592186263771 :answer/value "k=8"] [17592186264142 :answer/value "k=-12"] [17592186264279 :answer/value "k=7"] [17592186264722 :answer/value "k=-14"] [17592186266117 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=-1 \\\\ 4x+3ky &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186266117 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3k & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3k & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3k+12 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=-1 \\\\ (3k+12)y &=7.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=7,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$"] [17592186263361 :answer/value "k=-13"] [17592186264698 :answer/value "k=7"] [17592186264775 :answer/value "k=-3"] [17592186264929 :answer/value "k=-12"] [17592186265163 :answer/value "k=-4"] [17592186266118 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+4y &=-2 \\\\ -3x+(3k+15)y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186266118 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 3k+15 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 3k+15 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3k+3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+4y &=-2 \\\\ (3k+3)y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-1,$ then the second equation is $0=2,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-1.$"] [17592186264216 :answer/value "k=1"] [17592186264291 :answer/value "k=-1"] [17592186264336 :answer/value "k=-2"] [17592186264662 :answer/value "k=0"] [17592186264822 :answer/value "k=2"] [17592186266119 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+7y &=6 \\\\ x+ky &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186266119 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 & 6 \\\\ 1 & k & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 & 6 \\\\ 1 & k & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & -3 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2k+7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+ky &=-3 \\\\ (2k+7)y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=-\\frac{7}{2},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=-3.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=-\\frac{7}{2}.$"] [17592186263588 :answer/value "k=-\\frac{2}{7}"] [17592186263720 :answer/value "k=-7"] [17592186264099 :answer/value "k=\\frac{7}{2}"] [17592186264578 :answer/value "k=-\\frac{7}{2}"] [17592186264875 :answer/value "k=0"] [17592186266120 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+6y &=-4 \\\\ 5x+(k-6)y &=10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186266120 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & k-6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & k-6 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & k+9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+6y &=-4 \\\\ (k+9)y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=-9,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y.$ So, we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $-2x+6y=-4.$  \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=-9.$"] [17592186263711 :answer/value "k=-9"] [17592186264442 :answer/value "k=-6"] [17592186264621 :answer/value "k=0"] [17592186264666 :answer/value "k=9"] [17592186264838 :answer/value "k=-12"] [17592186302109 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y &=3 \\\\ 4x+ky &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186302109 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & k & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & k+2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y &=3 \\\\ (k+2)y &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nNow if $k=-2$, then the second equation is $0=-2,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when  $k=-2.$"] [17592186300764 :answer/value "k=0"] [17592186300773 :answer/value "k=-4"] [17592186300778 :answer/value "k=2"] [17592186300857 :answer/value "k=-2"] [17592186301152 :answer/value "k=4"] [17592186302143 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x-y &=2 \\\\ 8x+ky &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186302143 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & k & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x-y &=2 \\\\ (k-2)y &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=2,$ then the second equation is $0=9,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=2.$"] [17592186300241 :answer/value "k=2"] [17592186300297 :answer/value "k=0"] [17592186300560 :answer/value "k=-5"] [17592186300642 :answer/value "k=-8"] [17592186300734 :answer/value "k=-2"] [17592186344961 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=2 \\\\ 2x+(k+1)y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186344961 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=2 \\\\ (k-7)y &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=7,$ then the second equation is $0=-1,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=7.$"] [17592186343154 :answer/value "k=5"] [17592186343221 :answer/value "k=3"] [17592186343724 :answer/value "k=1"] [17592186343764 :answer/value "k=9"] [17592186344356 :answer/value "k=7"] [17592186344969 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=-4 \\\\ 3x+2ky &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186344969 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2k & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2k & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2k-6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=-4 \\\\ (2k-6)y &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=3,$ then the second equation is $0=10,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=3.$"] [17592186342643 :answer/value "k=-2"] [17592186343786 :answer/value "k=-1"] [17592186343902 :answer/value "k=-3"] [17592186344066 :answer/value "k=3"] [17592186344130 :answer/value "k=2"] [17592186344971 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y &=-8 \\\\ x+ky &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186344971 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & k & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & k & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2-4k & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+ky &=2 \\\\ (2-4k)y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{1}{2},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=2.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186342339 :answer/value "k=\\frac{1}{4}"] [17592186342833 :answer/value "k=-\\frac{1}{4}"] [17592186343257 :answer/value "k=\\frac{1}{2}"] [17592186344317 :answer/value "k=2"] [17592186344440 :answer/value "k=\\frac{1}{8}"] [17592186398982 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y &=4 \\\\ x+(k+1)y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186398982 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & k+1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & k+1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & k-2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y &=4 \\\\ (k-2)y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=2,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y.$ So, we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $2x+6y=4.$  \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=2.$"] [17592186398984 :answer/value "k=\\frac{1}{2}"] [17592186398985 :answer/value "k=-\\frac{1}{2}"] [17592186398986 :answer/value "k=-2"] [17592186398987 :answer/value "k=2"] [17592186398988 :answer/value "k=-4"] [17592186417082 :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=12 \\\\ x+ky &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186417082 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix 