erefore, the solution is $x=0$, $y=-7$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,-7,3⟩.$"] [17592186263750 :answer/value "⟨-14,7,3⟩"] [17592186264124 :answer/value "⟨0,7,3⟩"] [17592186264225 :answer/value "⟨4,-11,3⟩"] [17592186264365 :answer/value "⟨-14,-7,3⟩"] [17592186265030 :answer/value "⟨0,-7,3⟩"] [17592186287990 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186287990 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=-3.$ Substituting this into the second equation,  we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(-3)=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2-2(-3)=-2+6=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(4)+3(-3)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1-8+9=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=0$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=-3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186285617 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286009 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286231 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286438 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286929 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186341889 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186341889 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z &=-8 \\\\ y+2z &=-1 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+2(2)=-1\\,⟹\\,y=-1-4=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=2$ and $y=-5$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+2(-5)+(2)=-8\\,⟹\\,x=-8+10-2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=0$, $y=-5$, and $z=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,-5,2⟩.$"] [17592186339340 :answer/value "⟨-2,-4,2⟩"] [17592186340374 :answer/value "⟨0,-5,2⟩"] [17592186340800 :answer/value "⟨0,-8,0⟩"] [17592186340981 :answer/value "⟨1,-3,-3⟩"] [17592186341101 :answer/value "⟨-2,-1,-4⟩"] [17592186341890 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186341890 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this into the second equation,  we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+3(-1)=-4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-4+3=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(-1)+4(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3+1+4=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=-1$, and ${x}_{3}=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186339730 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186339973 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186339974 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186340520 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186340665 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186096458 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations that is represented by the augmented matrix\n![](images/2f/2fdf3fdd1bcbd2d7133c5e7a124cbe5e28d9eb4429affeb7376178984207ee4b.svg)"] [17592186096458 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n![](images/d3/d319f3db5d82a2076a53b04ac838cc811c9b4adfd65493d5197bf94befd11d44.svg)\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n3x_3=3 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \n\tx_3=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(1)=-2\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_2=-2-1=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $x_3 = 1$ and $x_2 = -3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x_1+(-3)-(1) = 0\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2} (3 + 1) = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x_1 = 2,$ $x_2=-3,$ and $x_3 = 1.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 2,-3,1 \\rangle.$"] [17592186096923 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186093513 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186093514 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186093515 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186093516 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186093517 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186096924 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z &=10 \\\\ y+5z &=15 \\\\ -z &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186096924 :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n-z=5\\,⟹\\,z=-5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+5(-5)=15\\,⟹\\,y=15+25=40.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-5$ and $y=40$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+2(40)-(-5)=10\\,⟹\\,x=10-80-5=-75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-75,y=40$ and $z=-5.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-75,40,-5⟩.$"] [17592186236335 :answer/value "⟨-15,40,-5⟩"] [17592186236375 :answer/value "⟨-85,40,-5⟩"] [17592186236570 :answer/value "⟨-75,40,-5⟩"] [17592186236790 :answer/value "⟨-5,-10,5⟩"] [17592186236981 :answer/value "⟨-75,-10,-5⟩"] [17592186237870 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186237870 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y+z &=8 \\\\ y-z &=-1 \\\\ z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-0=-1\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=0$ and $y=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x-2(-1)+(0)=8\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{8-2}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3$, $y=-1$, and $z=0.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨3,-1,0⟩.$"] [17592186236428 :answer/value "⟨2,-1,0⟩"] [17592186236657 :answer/value "⟨0,-1,8⟩"] [17592186236791 :answer/value "⟨2,-2,0⟩"] [17592186236847 :answer/value "⟨0,1,-8⟩"] [17592186237056 :answer/value "⟨3,-1,0⟩"] [17592186265751 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186265751 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+y+2z &=-3 \\\\ y+3z &=0 \\\\ -z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that \n\n$$\n-z=1\\,⟹\\,z=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(-1)=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-1$ and $y=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n4x+(3)+2(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{-3-3+2}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$, $y=3$, and $z=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186263410 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}."] [17592186263903 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}."] [17592186263938 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186265105 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}."] [17592186265115 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}."] [17592186265752 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -{x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186265752 :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n-{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{3}=1.\n$$\n\n Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-3-2=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=-5$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-(-5)+4(1)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{-1-5-4}{2}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n${x}_{1}=-5$, ${x}_{2}=-5$, and ${x}_{3}=1.$  In vector form, the solution can be written as $\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186263151 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186263351 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186263853 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264309 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264632 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186265753 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=7 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186265753 :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n2{x}_{3}=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\n Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}+3(2)=7\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=6-7=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+5(-1)+(2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1+5-2=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=-1$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$  In vector form, the solution can be written as $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186263706 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264015 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264025 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264319 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264748 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186341891 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-z &=2 \\\\ y+3z &=6 \\\\ -z &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186341891 :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that\n\n$$\n-z=-3\\,⟹\\,z=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(3)=6\\,⟹\\,y=6-9=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-3$ into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n2x+(-3)-(3)=2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}(2+3+3)=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=4$, $y=-3$ and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨4,-3,3⟩.$"] [17592186339143 :answer/value "⟨1,3,-3⟩"] [17592186339815 :answer/value "⟨2,-4,3⟩"] [17592186340578 :answer/value "⟨3,4,-3⟩"] [17592186341106 :answer/value "⟨1,-3,3⟩"] [17592186341119 :answer/value "⟨4,-3,3⟩"] [17592186341892 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186341892 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+z &=2 \\\\ 3y+2z &=-1 \\\\ -z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n-z=2\\,⟹\\,z=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n3y+2(-2)=-1\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}(-1+4)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-2$ and $y=1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-2)=2\\,⟹\\,x=2+2=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=4$, $y=1$, and $z=-2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186339326 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186340049 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186340833 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186341099 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186341217 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186341893 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186341893 :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+z &=-1 \\\\ y+z &=1 \\\\ 2z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n \n\n$$\n2z=1\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(\\frac{1}{2})=1\\,⟹\\,y=1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=y=\\frac{1}{2}$  into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x+(\\frac{1}{2})+(\\frac{1}{2})=-1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}(-1-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$, $y=\\frac{1}{2}$, and $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.$"] [17592186339616 :answer/value "⟨1,\\frac{1}{2},1⟩"] [17592186339702 :answer/value "⟨-1,-1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩"] [17592186339946 :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩"] [17592186340272 :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2},-1⟩"] [17592186340752 :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},-1⟩"] [17592186060941 :tutorial/content "Consider the following system of linear equations and its respective **augmented matrix**:\n![](images/2c/2c5dfe046037750f98f773ad906094f5ffbad3c285adf200134a119f72888ac8.svg)\n\nIf we can convert the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form, then we can solve the system using back substitution.\n\nThe procedure for reducing $M$ to row echelon form using elementary row operations is called **Gaussian elimination**. To perform Gaussian elimination on a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we use the following procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Consider the entry $a_{11}=2$ of the coefficient matrix. This entry is called a **pivot element**. We want to make this entry equal to $1$. So, the first elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Consider the entry $a_{21}=3$ below the first pivot element. We want to make this entry equal to $0$. So, the second elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_2 :=R_2+(-3)R_1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+({\\color{blue}-3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & -10 & 5\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, consider the entry $a_{22}=-10$. This entry is the second pivot element, and we would like to make it equal to $1$. So, the third elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_2 :=\\dfrac{1}{-10}R_2\n$$\n\nand we get $M$ in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{red}-10} & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{{\\color{red}-10}}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{red}1} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n![](images/b8/b83c4646349bada803311e5e9e160aafc8f08e6f51c7b69302def5ca4eeaa3fa.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3 \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = -1 \n\t\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\tx=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left< \\dfrac{1}{2}, -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right>.$"] [17592186059584 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n![](images/46/464ba24026d89efb3e62f7807426e5c413f277da3c8087931d2e3fde9a0b8e74.svg)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix."] [17592186059584 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n![](images/ba/baf05a693deacc3ef644165bae4de7b1435a90c73f050ac76c48892b2e010393.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=1$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2(1)=0 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-2$ and $y=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left<  -2,1 \\right>.$"] [17592186059965 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$M = \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -9 & -21 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 5\\end{array}\\right]$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form."] [17592186056150 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056151 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056152 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -9 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056153 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186056154 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -9 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186059978 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$M = \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9 & 5\\end{array}\\right]$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix."] [17592186056227 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]$,\n$x = 1$ and $y =-1$"] [17592186056228 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]$,\n$x = 1$ and $y =-1$"] [17592186056229 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]$,\n$x = 7$ and $y =-1$"] [17592186056230 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]$,\n$x = 7$ and $y =-1$"] [17592186056231 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]$,\n$x =-3$ and $y = 1$"] [17592186237988 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form."] [17592186237988 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form using the standard algorithm for Gaussian elimination, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{13}{2} & -\\frac{13}{2}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{13}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235879 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186235912 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186236215 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186236421 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186237357 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{7}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186241137 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix."] [17592186241137 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 11\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-1\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -11\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=8 \\\\ y &=-11,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-11$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(-11)=8\\,⇒\\,x=8+11=19.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=19$ and $y=-11.$"] [17592186238272 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 14\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=13$ and $y=14$"] [17592186238893 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 13\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=11$ and $y=13$"] [17592186239691 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -11\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=19$ and $y=-11$"] [17592186239803 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -16\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=17$ and $y=-16$"] [17592186240071 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 11\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=10$ and $y=11$"] [17592186241149 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 8\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form."] [17592186241149 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form using the standard algorithm for Gaussian elimination, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-5\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 16 & 13\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{13}{16}\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186238587 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{11}{16}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186238598 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{13}{16}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186239014 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{15}{16}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186239668 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{17}{16}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186240258 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{9}{16}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186266010 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 10 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix."] [17592186266010 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 10 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+7y &=-2 \\\\ y &=-1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-1$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+7(-1)=-2\\,⇒\\,x=-2+7=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5$ and $y=-1.$"] [17592186263066 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right],$ $\\,x=12$ and $y=-2$"] [17592186263833 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right],$ $\\,x=13$ and $y=-1$"] [17592186263915 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right],$ $\\,x=15$ and $y=1$"] [17592186263968 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right],$ $\\,x=5$ and $y=-1$"] [17592186264512 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right],$ $\\,x=12$ and $y=2$"] [17592186287991 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -6 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix."] [17592186287991 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -6 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ y &=-\\frac{5}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-\\frac{5}{3}$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2(-\\frac{5}{3})=\\frac{10}{3}\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{10}{3}-\\frac{10}{3}=0.\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=0$ and $y=-\\frac{5}{3}.$"] [17592186285062 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=0$ and $y=\\frac{5}{3}$"] [17592186285447 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{3}$"] [17592186285793 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-\\frac{8}{3}$ and $y=-3$"] [17592186286805 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=0$ and $y=-\\frac{5}{3}$"] [17592186286892 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=\\frac{20}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{5}{3}$"] [17592186287992 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix."] [17592186287992 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 1 & -2 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-3(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{9}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=-3$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186285289 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{17}{2}\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-1$ and $y=-\\frac{17}{2}$"] [17592186286019 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-3$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}$"] [17592186286626 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=3$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$"] [17592186286761 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{17}{2}\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=\\frac{4}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{15}{2}$"] [17592186286951 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{9}{2}\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\frac{9}{2}$"] [17592186386127 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -8\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form."] [17592186386127 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form using the standard algorithm for Gaussian elimination, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 6 & 4 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 8 & -16\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386129 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186386130 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186386131 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186386132 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186386133 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186388195 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form."] [17592186388195 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form using the standard algorithm for Gaussian elimination, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388197 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186388198 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186388199 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186388200 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186388201 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186060942 :tutorial/content "The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique. In the previous example, after the first row operation we arrived at \n\n![](images/eb/ebcb9bc5aedf609284436ad1c10e98f9b22d91a382136b5aabfb3f18c736ddae.svg)\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n![](images/53/536245bdc7fc9dab02723d3576adff774dfd9711551f472333c027d83f76c05b.svg)\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier."] [17592186059585 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/7b/7b1adc08164fdcf1a5322b4fbfd3e5b0f57e22e018aa801add4bb3b572c29f76.svg)\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186059585 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/87/8773defc4db521e846b58b7c5ef04b54f26723d94099c75caffbeca0587c33dc.svg)\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n![](images/45/455dc49a531a371ea8aecd6259a677c8006bb2a23181ed66d27721135f240c89.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-10$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-5(-10)=-5 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-55.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-55$ and $y=-10.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -55,-10 \\rangle.$"] [17592186059970 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=-8 \\\\ 2x+4y &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186059970 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=-8 \\\\ y &=2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=2$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-3(2)=-8\\,⇒\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-2$ and $y=2.$"] [17592186238787 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-4$ and $y=1$"] [17592186239148 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-8$ and $y=2$"] [17592186240208 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-2$ and $y=2$"] [17592186240213 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-4$ and $y=1$"] [17592186240309 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-2$ and $y=2$"] [17592186060205 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+7y &=-5 \\\\ 7x+11y &=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186060205 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{7}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{7}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{27}{2} & \\frac{27}{2}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{27}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{7}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186285671 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{7}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186286170 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186286517 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{7}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186286718 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186286971 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{7}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186287994 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+8y &=22 \\\\ 7x-5y &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186287994 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 8 & 22 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 8 & 22 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{8}{3} & \\frac{22}{3} \\\\ 7 & -5 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{8}{3} & \\frac{22}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{71}{3} & -\\frac{142}{3}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{3}{71}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{8}{3} & \\frac{22}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186285038 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{8}{3} & \\frac{22}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186285438 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{8}{3} & \\frac{22}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186286195 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{8}{3} & \\frac{22}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186286223 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{8}{3} & \\frac{22}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186286571 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{8}{3} & \\frac{22}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186287995 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{1}{3}y &=11 \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{5}y &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186287995 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 11 \\\\ 3 & \\frac{1}{5} & 9\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 11 \\\\ 3 & \\frac{1}{5} & 9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 11 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{4}{5} & -24\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{5}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 11 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 30\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{1}{3}y &=11 \\\\ y &=30,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=30$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+\\frac{1}{3}(30)=11\\,⇒\\,x=11-10=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1$ and $y=30.$"] [17592186284851 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 11 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=12$ and $y=-3$"] [17592186285992 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 11 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=9$ and $y=6$"] [17592186286208 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 11 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=10$ and $y=3$"] [17592186286934 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 11 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 30\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=1$ and $y=30$"] [17592186286994 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{1}{3} & 11 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=11$ and $y=0$"] [17592186342013 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+12y &=-4 \\\\ 5x+11y &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186342013 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186339149 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186339677 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186340461 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186340499 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186341077 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186388202 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+5y &=-6 \\\\ -6x-6y &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186388202 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 10\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & 5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{5}{3} & -2 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{5}{3} & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{5}{3} & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388204 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{5}{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186388205 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186388206 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{5}{3} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186388207 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & \\frac{5}{3} & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186388208 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186393299 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-2y &=4 \\\\ 2x-y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186393299 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393301 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186393302 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186393303 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186393304 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186393305 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186393306 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4y &=6 \\\\ 4x-7y &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186393306 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 10\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=3 \\\\ y &=-2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-2$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2(-2)=3\\,⇒\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=-1$ and $y=-2.$"] [17592186393308 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=1$ and $y=-1$"] [17592186393309 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-1$ and $y=-2$"] [17592186393310 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=-5$ and $y=-4$"] [17592186393311 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=7$ and $y=2$"] [17592186393312 :answer/value "$M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right],$\n$\\,x=11$ and $y=4$"] [17592186393313 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-15y &=10 \\\\ 2x-4y &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186393313 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}5 & -15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393315 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186393316 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186393317 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186393318 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186393319 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186414018 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-8y &=10 \\\\ 3x-7y &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186414018 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 5\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186414012 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186414013 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186414014 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186414015 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186414016 :answer/value "M∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]"] [17592186097841 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/67/673ea82819858b9a46e0e351a8d6f41b069ed039ee0d31a382110f938f7ef72a.svg)\n\nWe can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination algorithm. The algorithm that we use is very similar to the case of $2 \\times 2$ systems. \n\nOur goal is to create a descending \"staircase\" of $1$'s with $0$'s below, and we achieve this using elementary row operations. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & -24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix is now in row echelon form, and we can apply back substitution to solve the original system of equations.\n\n**Note:** The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique.  In the previous example, after the second row operation we arrived at\n\n![](images/35/35627a034b30736abb057c01f3251fc047dce780a8cbcf389f8b5c2c2317d766.svg)\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n![](images/32/32a252e05e299a206f2f3776a2a04419f21726e1f8f542cfd4f328dac3251f21.svg)\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier."] [17592186097842 :tutorial/content "The **standard Gaussian elimination** for a $3 \\times 3$ system can be described as follows:\n\n- If $a_{11} \\neq 0$ and $a_{11} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1/a_{11}.$\n\n- We use first row and the necessary row operations to make the remaining entries of the first column below $a_{11}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n- if $a_{21} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-a_{21})R_1;$\n\n- if $a_{31} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3 + (-a_{31})R_1.$\n\n- If $a_{22} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2/a_{22}.$\n\n- We use the second row and the necessary row operation to make the entry below $a_{22}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n- if $a_{32} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3 + (-a_{32})R_2.$\n\n- If $a_{33} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3/a_{33}.$\n\n- In some exceptional cases, such as when the element on the diagonal is equal to $0,$ we first need to exchange rows. Then proceed using the same steps as above.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to row echelon form."] [17592186096459 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n![](images/b3/b3512e604d6c4246c5582a75360076115c4bd9b6701c42587f318254b1c026a3.svg)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186096459 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/76/765eafb8a8b60aec9af512eeb666e2e13dbf83b8bc331b6effa75143930b4892.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $z=2$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+(2) &=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting both $y=-4$ and $z=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-4)+(2) &=1\\,⟹\\,x=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-5,$ $y=-4,$ and $z=2.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -5,-4,2 \\rangle.$"] [17592186096937 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 25\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186096937 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 25\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=19 \\\\ y+2z &=13 \\\\ z &=5,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $z=5$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+2(5)=13\\,⟹\\,y=13-10=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting both $y=3$ and $z=5$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(3)+2(5)=19\\,⟹\\,x=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=6,y=3$, and $z=5.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨6,3,5⟩.$"] [17592186414020 :answer/value "⟨6,3,2⟩"] [17592186414021 :answer/value "⟨-3,1,2⟩"] [17592186414024 :answer/value "⟨1,2,5⟩"] [17592186414027 :answer/value "⟨6,3,5⟩"] [17592186414028 :answer/value "⟨-1,2,5⟩"] [17592186097183 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+6z &=5 \\\\ y-10z &=-11 \\\\ 4x+8y+13z &=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186097183 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 13 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 13 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{11}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186284826 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286487 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286562 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286868 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186287011 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186266014 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 3 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186266014 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 3 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z &=-3 \\\\ y-2z &=-2 \\\\ z &=8,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $z=8$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-2(8)=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-2+16=14.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting both $y=14$ and $z=8$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2(14)+(8)=-3\\,⟹\\,x=17.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=17,y=14$, and $z=8.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨17,14,8⟩.$"] [17592186263444 :answer/value "⟨17,6,8⟩"] [17592186264647 :answer/value "⟨-1,2,2⟩"] [17592186265143 :answer/value "⟨5,14,8⟩"] [17592186265178 :answer/value "⟨17,14,8⟩"] [17592186265230 :answer/value "⟨5,6,4⟩"] [17592186287983 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=6 \\\\ y+z &=5 \\\\ 2x+3y+2z &=14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and hence solve the linear system."] [17592186287983 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 & 14\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 & 14\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=6 \\\\ y+z &=5 \\\\ z &=3,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $z=3$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(3)=5\\,⟹\\,y=5-3=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting both $y=2$ and $z=3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(2)+(3)=6\\,⟹\\,x=6-5=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1,y=2$ and $z=3.$"] [17592186285033 :answer/value "$x=1,y=8$ and $z=-3$"] [17592186285735 :answer/value "$x=1,y=4$ and $z=1$"] [17592186286213 :answer/value "$x=1,y=6$ and $z=-1$"] [17592186286234 :answer/value "$x=1,y=2$ and $z=3$"] [17592186286509 :answer/value "$x=1,y=5$ and $z=0$"] [17592186287997 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 6 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186287997 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 6 & 21\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186284977 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286091 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286504 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286742 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186287014 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186287998 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=0 \\\\ y+2z &=0 \\\\ x+y+3z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186287998 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186285001 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186285352 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186285424 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186285870 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186286652 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186287999 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=-3 \\\\ x+2y+2z &=2 \\\\ 3x+4y+4z &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and hence solve the linear system."] [17592186287999 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 17\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=-3 \\\\ y &=5 \\\\ z &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. Substituting both $y=5$ and $z=-6$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+5+2(-6)=-3\\,⟹\\,x-7=-3\\,⟹\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=4,$ $y=5,$ and $z=-6.$"] [17592186284870 :answer/value "$x=-11,y=4$ and $z=4$"] [17592186285312 :answer/value "$x=-1,y=4$ and $z=-2$"] [17592186286436 :answer/value "$x=13,y=-4$ and $z=-4$"] [17592186286806 :answer/value "$x=-2,y=4$ and $z=2$"] [17592186286999 :answer/value "$x=4,y=5$ and $z=-6$"] [17592186367935 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z &=-4 \\\\ 2x+5y+3z &=-10 \\\\ 3x+6y+4z &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186367935 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3 & -10 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 & -12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3 & -10 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367937 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186367938 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186367939 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186367940 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186367941 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186368702 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 12 & 36 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186368702 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 12 & 36 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 12 & 36 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 12 & 36 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z &=1 \\\\ y-3z &=-9 \\\\ z &=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $z=1$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-3(1)=-9\\,⟹\\,y=-6.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting both $y=-6$ and $z=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-(-6)+2(1)=1\\,⟹\\,x=-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-7,y=-6$, and $z=1.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨-7,-6,1⟩.$"] [17592186368704 :answer/value "⟨-9,-12,-1⟩"] [17592186368705 :answer/value "⟨-8,-9,0⟩"] [17592186368706 :answer/value "⟨-7,-6,1⟩"] [17592186368707 :answer/value "⟨-6,-3,2⟩"] [17592186368708 :answer/value "⟨-10,-15,-2⟩"] [17592186390367 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186390367 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390369 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390370 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390371 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390372 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390373 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390374 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 9 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186390374 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 9 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390376 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390377 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390378 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390379 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390380 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186395769 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=1 \\\\ 2x+3y+4z &=6 \\\\ 3x+3y+4z &=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and hence solve the linear system."] [17592186395769 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=1 \\\\ y &=4 \\\\ z &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. Substituting both $y=4$ and $z=-4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(4)+2(-4)=1\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5,y=4$, and $z=-4.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨5,4,-4⟩.$"] [17592186395771 :answer/value "⟨1,4,-2⟩"] [17592186395772 :answer/value "⟨3,2,-2⟩"] [17592186395773 :answer/value "⟨5,4,-4⟩"] [17592186395774 :answer/value "⟨13,-4,-4⟩"] [17592186395775 :answer/value "⟨-11,4,4⟩"] [17592186096460 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/cd/cd75b39fc76773c0b3938ab6d60651e2b08dcd9ac325cc05718837934130ea07.svg)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186096460 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/93/933b897b7bc067610c87ea50a6fb871f04c5cc4610bd5bb0c868e6ffbac25e86.svg)\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get $a_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/42/42a1d61517661ccff31d9e603df548e79db7b0674a1f7f34c269a3a19e1c4bd6.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=1$ tells us the value of $z,$ and the second equation $y=3$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting these values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+(3)+(1) &=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-6,$ $y=3,$ and $z=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -6,3,1 \\rangle.$"] [17592186097349 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z &=8 \\\\ -2x+2y-3z &=-11 \\\\ x+z &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186097349 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z &=8 \\\\ y-z &=1 \\\\ z &=5,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=5$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(5) &=1\\,⟹\\,y=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=6$ and $z=5$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(6)+2(5) &=8\\,⟹\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=4$, $y=6$, and $z=5.$"] [17592186339638 :answer/value "$x=4$, $y=6$, and $z=5$"] [17592186340305 :answer/value "$x=-1$, $y=2$, and $z=5$"] [17592186340507 :answer/value "$x=4$, $y=-5$, and $z=2$"] [17592186341062 :answer/value "$x=4$, $y=5$, and $z=2$"] [17592186341142 :answer/value "$x=1$, $y=-6$, and $z=-5$"] [17592186097350 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186097350 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the $2$nd row by $-\\frac{1}{3}$ and the $3$rd row by $\\frac{1}{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186096025 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186096026 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186096027 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186096028 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186096029 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186403628 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186403629 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186403630 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186403631 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186403632 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186266015 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 & -12 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186266015 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 & -12 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the $2$nd row by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and the $3$rd row by $\\frac{1}{7}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{7}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186263727 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186263931 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264714 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264721 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 14 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186264872 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -7\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186342020 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+6y-3z &=18 \\\\ -4x+8y-3z &=2 \\\\ y-4z &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186342020 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & -3 & 18 \\\\ -4 & 8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & -3 & 18 \\\\ -4 & 8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ -4 & 8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -22\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z &=-6 \\\\ y-4z &=-3 \\\\ z &=-22,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=-22$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4(-22) &=-3\\,⟹\\,y=-91.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-91$ and $z=-22$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2(-91)+(-22) &=-6\\,⟹\\,x=-166.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=-166$, $y=-91$, and $z=-22.$"] [17592186339832 :answer/value "$x=9$, $y=9$, and $z=3$"] [17592186340027 :answer/value "$x=-166$, $y=-91$, and $z=-22$"] [17592186340306 :answer/value "$x=-89$, $y=-47$, and $z=-11$"] [17592186340724 :answer/value "$x=-33$, $y=-15$, and $z=-3$"] [17592186340897 :answer/value "$x=142$, $y=85$, and $z=22$"] [17592186342022 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186342022 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z &=3 \\\\ y+5z &=-8 \\\\ z &=4,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=4$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+5(4) &=-8\\,⟹\\,y=-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-28$ and $z=4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+(-28)-2(4) &=3\\,⟹\\,x=39.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=39$, $y=-28$, and $z=4.$ Or, in vector form $⟨39,-28,4⟩.$"] [17592186339203 :answer/value "⟨11,-8,0⟩"] [17592186339272 :answer/value "⟨39,-28,4⟩"] [17592186339463 :answer/value "⟨25,-18,2⟩"] [17592186339575 :answer/value "⟨-3,2,-2⟩"] [17592186340208 :answer/value "⟨-17,12,-4⟩"] [17592186342023 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 16 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186342023 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 16 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 16 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z &=-2 \\\\ y+2z &=-1 \\\\ z &=11,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=11$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2(11) &=-1\\,⟹\\,y=-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-23$ and $z=11$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-23)+3(11) &=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=-58$, $y=-23$, and $z=11.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨-58,-23,11⟩.$"] [17592186339543 :answer/value "⟨52,21,-11⟩"] [17592186339549 :answer/value "⟨-3,-1,0⟩"] [17592186339602 :answer/value "⟨-2,-3,1⟩"] [17592186339924 :answer/value "⟨-58,-23,11⟩"] [17592186341171 :answer/value "⟨2,1,-1⟩"] [17592186342025 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-6y+3z &=-6 \\\\ 2x-4y+3z &=-1 \\\\ 2y-4z &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186342025 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the $2$nd row by $\\frac{1}{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186339179 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186339595 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186339614 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186340757 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186340818 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186368709 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186368709 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=1 \\\\ y-z &=-4 \\\\ z &=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=1$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(1) &=-4\\,⟹\\,y=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3(-3) &=1\\,⟹\\,x=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=-8$, $y=-3$, and $z=1.$"] [17592186368711 :answer/value "$x=13$, $y=4$, and $z=0$"] [17592186368712 :answer/value "$x=13$, $y=6$, and $z=2$"] [17592186368713 :answer/value "$x=-8$, $y=-3$, and $z=1$"] [17592186368714 :answer/value "$x=10$, $y=3$, and $z=-1$"] [17592186368715 :answer/value "$x=16$, $y=5$, and $z=1$"] [17592186400513 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z &=4 \\\\ -3x-6y+4z &=-8 \\\\ 3x+7y-z &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system."] [17592186400513 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z &=4 \\\\ y+2z &=-2 \\\\ z &=4,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=4$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2(4) &=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-10$ and $z=4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(-10)-(4) &=4\\,⟹\\,x=28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=28$, $y=-10$, and $z=4.$"] [17592186400515 :answer/value "$x=-12$, $y=6$, and $z=-4$"] [17592186400516 :answer/value "$x=28$, $y=-10$, and $z=4$"] [17592186400517 :answer/value "$x=-12$, $y=-10$, and $z=2$"] [17592186400518 :answer/value "$x=28$, $y=-6$, and $z=4$"] [17592186400519 :answer/value "$x=18$, $y=6$, and $z=2$"] [17592186403654 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 20 & -10 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186403654 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 20 & -10 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the $2$nd row by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and the $3$rd row by $\\frac{1}{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186403656 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186403657 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186403658 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -9 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186403659 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186403660 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186414031 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+6y-3z &=-1 \\\\ -2x-12y+8z &=1 \\\\ -x-5y+4z &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form."] [17592186414031 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -12 & 8 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -12 & 8 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the $3$rd row by $\\frac{1}{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186414019 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186414022 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186414023 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186414025 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186414026 :answer/value "M∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186121051 :tutorial/content "Any plane can be uniquely determined by a point and two non-parallel direction vectors. For example, let $\\Pi$ be the plane that passes through the point $P(1,0,2)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 0,3,0 \\rangle$  and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle -1,1,1 \\rangle.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea4ce822abec2b68d2471a2787f4e045493ca43c1c6795a7e0069de80cb5adee.png)\n\nUsing the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c},$ we can construct a **parametric equation** of the plane.\n\nFirst, let $R$ be any general point in the plane, with position vector $\\mathbf{r}.$ Notice that we can get from $P$ to $R$ using a linear combination of $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ with some (unknown for us) coefficients $s$ and $t$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b40f91c3d75f092c7b29f19770364e7518d37b64ba594a311a762ad79a950e4f.png)\n\nSo, we can write the **parametric equation** of the plane as\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{r}} = \\mathbf{p}+s{\\color{blue}\\,\\mathbf{b}}+t\\,{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{c}},\n\t\\qquad s,t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThis formula works whenever $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are not parallel.\n\nIn our case, $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are indeed not parallel to each other. So, using column vector notation, the parametric equation for the plane $\\Pi$ is given by\n![](images/0d/0d40f8f87e1ed3f3bf5040e925e545169f5528f32cf8dd0052375aa560cc1381.svg)"] [17592186119647 :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,5)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 17,3,15 \\rangle$  and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle 5,0,-8 \\rangle.$"] [17592186119647 :question/worked-solution "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+ s\\mathbf{b}+ t \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,5 \\rangle,$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 17,3,15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle 5,0,-8 \\rangle.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-3,2,5⟩+s\\,⟨17,3,15⟩+t\\,⟨5,0,-8⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120234 :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-1,3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨1,3,0⟩$  and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,2,-0.5⟩.$"] [17592186120234 :question/worked-solution "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\nr=p+sb+tc,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $b$ and $c$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is $p=⟨1,-1,3⟩$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${v}_{1}=⟨1,3,0⟩$  and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,2,-0.5⟩.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨1,-1,3⟩+s⟨1,3,0⟩+t⟨0,2,-0.5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"] [17592186410862 :answer/value "r=s⟨1,3,0⟩+t⟨0,2,-0.5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186410863 :answer/value "r=⟨1,3,0⟩+s⟨1,-1,3⟩+t⟨0,2,-0.5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186410866 :answer/value "r=⟨0,2,-0.5⟩+s⟨1,3,0⟩+t⟨1,-1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186410880 :answer/value "None of the listed"] [17592186410884 :answer/value "r=⟨1,-1,3⟩+s⟨1,3,0⟩+t⟨0,2,-0.5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186120235 :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $A(4,-3, \\sqrt{2})$ and is parallel to the vectors $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186117570 :answer/value "r = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} + s\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)"] [17592186117571 :answer/value "r = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} + s\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)"] [17592186117572 :answer/value "r = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} + s\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)"] [17592186117573 :answer/value "None of the listed"] [17592186117574 :answer/value "r = s\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)"] [17592186262902 :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-3,5)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨2,4,1⟩$  and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,3,-1⟩.$"] [17592186262902 :question/worked-solution "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\nr=p+sb+tc,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $b$ and $c$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is $p=⟨2,-3,5⟩$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${v}_{1}=⟨2,4,1⟩$  and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,3,-1⟩.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨2,-3,5⟩+s⟨2,4,1⟩+t⟨1,3,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"] [17592186260340 :answer/value "r=⟨2,5,3⟩+s⟨-2,4,1⟩+t⟨3,1,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186260600 :answer/value "r=⟨2,3,5⟩+s⟨1,-4,2⟩+t⟨1,3,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186260750 :answer/value "r=⟨2,-5,3⟩+s⟨2,4,1⟩+t⟨3,-1,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186262032 :answer/value "r=⟨2,-3,5⟩+s⟨2,4,1⟩+t⟨1,3,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186262098 :answer/value "r=⟨2,-5,3⟩+s⟨4,2,1⟩+t⟨1,3,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [17592186262903 :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-3,4)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨1,5,-1⟩$  and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,2.5,1⟩.$"] [17592186262903 :question/worked-solution "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\nr=p+sb+tc,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $b$ and $c$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\